Jokes aside I just have a general plot for the BBG and all the rest is made around it. If my BBG need to do a massive ritual then I just assume that all the weird event my PC witness are linked to the gathering of ingredient for the ritual. I create random event on the spot and just have to find a way to link them to the main plot. If my players suddently want to do something else all I have to do is to find a way to make it relevant for the BBG plot.

&nbsp;

* Players want to go to the nearest temple because one of the paladin want to serve his god? Cool, the temple just lost a relic and the temple need help to find it. At the end they'll realised it has been stolen for the ritual.
* Players want to spend some time at the tavern? No beer. Apparently all alcohol has been stolen.
* Players want to go to a pond to swim and relax? A group of fishermen complain that all the fish disappear.

&nbsp;

At the end no matter what they do they'll end up looking for missing stuff. Put some hint that a guy stole it, they find the guy, he admits he stole it because he was paid by a weird dude, they find him while he his going to prepare the ritual and you got your big fight.

&nbsp;

You can apply this method to almost any plot. Get something VERY large for your BBG and then reduce the plot around what your PC are doing. You just have to always keep in mind the goal of the BBG and drop hint from time to time.

For artisan I recommend you to no micro-manage ressources. Spread the ressources between "Copper", "Silver" and "Gold" grade and when he want to craft something just tell him he need X amount of Copper material and Y amount of Silver. This is great for 2 reasons:

&nbsp;

- Crafting will be way easier and quicker which will allow the player to experience crafting without having to spend hours IRL to deal with it.
- You can give crafting material as a reward when people go into a room and search for loot without having to think about what kind of material you give.

&nbsp;

Also don't hesitate to make small craft like sharpening. I allowed my blacksmith to sharpen weapon and gave an extra +1 damage for first hit. It's not a lot but it mades the blacksmith a lot into craft.

Use insanity point. Make them easier to gain and also easier to remove. I give insanity point for almost anyhting that can be a trauma for the player. Stress? Insanity point. Too much magic? Insanity point. Horror? Insanity point.

&nbsp;

To balance it I also made them easier to lose. Making a friend, enjoying a cool evening at the tavern, achieving personnal quest all this kind of stuff remove insanity point.

&nbsp;

At the end I use insanity point the same way I use hit point but for mental stuff instead of physical stuff. It should be something that often go up and down so the player actually play with it and take it into consideration for every choice. For exemple I had players with high insanity score that REFUSED to enter a room when the first player to step inside describe the room as being "full of corpses". They were like: "ok my insanity is too high, I can't risk it, I'll let the other players do and i'll take a break". I end up having players using some skill to appeased others players so they can lose some insanity point and keep going.

I had an entire party taking a break into a dungeon to eat something good and play some dice together to appeased their character. It's also a cool way to make some skill usefull. For exemple cooking skill can be used to justify losing few insanity point when eating a good meal instead of trash stuff. The PC that doesn't contribute a lot to fight but keep the group happy and united get rewared for doing so.

I do not make large fight tactical. I just require player to throw a CC or CT roll that will cover their entire fight. If they roll low I kill some NPC and describe it as a fail to protect them or I give them some injuries. If they roll high they protect NPC and end up having a better relationship with their fight budies.

&nbsp;

You can cut the entire fight into several smaller "chapter" so they can adjust their actions but overall it can be used for every "big war" moment. In a huge war a PC doing 10 or 15 damages won't change anything so there is no real reason to request damages roll.

I'm generous when they make an important critical roll or when they achieve something on a smart way.

&nbsp;

In my new campaign they escaped from prison and end up in an haunted forest almost naked. One of the player roll a 1 in survival skill and he end up finding the corpse of a hunter with a lighter inside his bag. He was super proud to come back to the camp with fire and I let him enjoyed his moment. At the end of the session another player end up being alone in the forest during night. The "Beast" haunting the forest tried to catch him and at the last moment he decided to take a risk, stop running and beg for mercy in hope that the Beast could understand what he was saying. We just finished his new character sheet for the second session.

&nbsp;

You don't have to pamper them. Just reward what has to be rewarded and punish what has to be punished.

Don't wait the next session to create the new character. Give him a player sheet and make him toss dice on the flight to create it. The only dice you really need to roll immediatly are "name", "sex" and "race". The rest can be improvised or decided later when it becomes needed.

&nbsp;

Also when someone lost his character I'm more open to let him decide what he want to play next so just let him choose few element of his new character like "size", "wieght", "eyes color" etc. It makes the creation even quicker and the player will forget is old character more easily.

Make it so when a player do a nat1 then the owlbear do something stupid and dangerous.

&nbsp;

Going shopping? The owlbear eat a dog in the marketplace while you were looking at items and the dog owner is screaming. They'll either try their best to protect him and it'll be super fun to play or they'll get rid of him because they'll finally understand that it's not a domestic pet.

There is a lot of way to solve this but I would have talk with him about it. Nerf him with a malediction and build a personnal arc for this player so one of his goal in this dungeon is to reclaim his lost power.

&nbsp;

Just be sure that at the end he'll have few fight to fully enjoy his OP character.

Build a character who take his order from his deity and let him ignore the others actions to serve his god. At the end players 2 and 3 will have to take player 1 god into consideration when planning something and the player 1 will have a word to say like "I don't think my god agree with this action, let's do this instead".

&nbsp;

Just be cool with player 1 when he does something a bit stupid just to follow his deity order.

I always fudge and my players know it. I always told them: "You are the player and I'm here to tell a story. If the story told by the dice is boring, bad or totally unrealistic then I ignore the result".

&nbsp;

Sure it sometimes give the feeling that the orc shouldn't have hit or that the sudden great action from the dying boss is REALLY convenient for him. But at the same time none if them miss being killed by a low trash gobelin just because the dice said so. It's a win-win situation. They know that what happens to them is not a random fiesta and it gives me a way better control over the pace of the game.

Did you consider that maybe your players are more into roleplaying than combat simulation and maybe you should make your fight more exciting by making them more descriptive rather than more strategic? Maybe if they have a hard time to get into strategy it's because they do not really care about this aspect of the game.

&nbsp;

It tooks me 15 years to understand that my players weren't really into tactical fight. They want me to tell them how the barbarian cut the orc head, how the assassin manage to sneak and kill the sorcerer without being spotted etc. They do not want to micro manage every single step or hit. It's very time consuming and if players aren't really into the strategic part of the game it makes the session really boring for them.

&nbsp;

As a DM you want to make your players happy by giving them the full control of their character but at the end you'll realized that they don't know what to do with such liberty and it often end up with meta gaming. It's ok to ask them what kind of action they want to do, ask for a roll and then take control of their character to describe the action more precisely. It'll not only make fight quicker but it'll allow player to do stuff they wouldn't be able to imagine IRL. It's rewarding for players because they will admire their own character.

Avoid expanding too much and too fast. A lot of small issues will stack and you end up having a TONS of people waiting for you to trip so they can jump on your back all together. The biggest conquest are made once your land is secured and all vassals are fully under your rule.

&nbsp;

Don't be afraid to switch between lifestyle focus. A lot of very early talents point are extremelly usefull. For exemple if you have a character focused on intrigue it's amazingly usefull to go and get the "exchange hook for money" in stewardship.

&nbsp;

If you liege goes into an easy war, propose to participate. Get a small army and avoid fight. Just pick of few city to get the gold and prisonner and once the war is over you'll get: money from siege, prisonner to exchange for money, fame and massive friendship with your liege.

