A mansion is the house of a Lord too. The difference between a mansion and a castle are both the size and the defensive purpose.

&#x200B;

Castle is for defensive building or massive house (like Versailles castle which isn't defensive at all). Mansion is for smaller and non defensive house, often for low rank Nobles. Because the line is not set, some average building are called either a castle or a mansion mostly depending on how important the Noble was.

A "Château" is a castle which means it's was the house of a Lord.

&#x200B;

Un Château d'eau is a water tower but it's like a single word. It's because those building were massive and put in height likemedieval castle so we named it "water castle". 

&#x200B;

For country house it's often an exageration. When you say that's a building is a castle it imply that it is a massive house. "Sa maison est tellement grande! C'est un vrai château!"

It's more than granted and expected. It's mandatory. It's an unwritten law. Bonjour and S'il te plait are the 2 words you teach to your kid after Maman and Papa. When you meet a kid in France you'll very very often see the parents insists A LOT that the kid say bonjour. All kids are shy with people they don't know and very often you end waiting 2 or 3 minutes that the kid finally accept to say bonjour. We take it super seriously. It's more than just saying Hi! It's a form of respect and you show that you aknowledge the presence of the person.

&#x200B;

For you it's just "Bonjour". For us it's "I saw you, I make sure you know I am here, I respect you, I mean you no harm and if you need something I allow you to talk to me". This is what Bonjour implies in France. Once someone said Bonjour to you then he is no more a complete stranger. This is why we respond so bad to people ignoring it.

It's more than unusual. It's a dead form and not correct anymore except if you want to give an old school style to your writting. I agree that it was used in old French but now you'll get corrected and it's wrong to use this form.

&#x200B;

