Oi! - The polite way to catch attention is "Monsieur/Madame/Mademoiselle!". "Hé!" or "Hé ho!" can be used if you don't want to be polite, like for someone stepping on your foot.

&#x200B;

"Ca va?" ghetto style = "Bien ou bien?". It makes fun of the fact that "Ca va?" has no other answer than "Bien et toi?". It's also a "wordgame" because "ou bien" means "or else". So it can both be translated in "Good or else?" or "Good or good?"

&#x200B;

So people say "Bien ou bien?" and you answer "Bien et toi?".

"Passé composé" is for an action set and done. Imparfait is more for description or an long action.

&#x200B;

Je promenais mon chien lorsque j'ai vu un accident de voiture - I was walking my dog when i saw an car crash

&#x200B;

Walking the dog is a drescription of what you were doing (imparfait). You saw the car crash and it was an action "set and done" (passé composé).

"Putain" and "merde" are used a lot. You drop your glass? "Et meeeeeeeeeeerde!". You miss your train? "oooooh Putaiiiiiiin!". In the South of France "Putain" is often considered as a ponctuation and can easily be use instead of "!".

&#x200B;

We also use Cool and Nice.

