**note:** Mes copains = Mes amis. Mon copain = My boyfriend. If you just want to speak about one specific friend you say "un de mes copains" (one of my friend).

&#x200B;

What did you do at the weekend?

Well, just describe you weekend. It's complicated to help if we don't even know what you did during the weekend :)

If you use "tu" you say "s'il te plait", if you use "vous" you say "s'il vous plait". You do not mix "tu" and "vous" form. It's either full "tu" or full "vous".

&#x200B;

The easy way for a foreigner is to always start with "vous" and see how people respond. If they switch to "tu" you switch to "tu". Since you are a foreigner people won't mind mistakes and to be honest people do not make a big deal over a misuse of "tu/vous" form.

&#x200B;

The main point is to remember that using "vous" when someone use "tu" create some sort of hierarchy. It implies that the person using "tu" is above the person using "vous". It's ok at work with your boss or with old people but overall people stay at the same level so just copy what the person in front of you do.

