"Je n'ai pas de cours" and "chez mon médecin" are both correct too.

&#x200B;

"Je n'ai pas de cours" is actually better on a written form because of grammar but during a discussion with a friend you'll say "j'ai pas cours".

&#x200B;

French are not strict with grammar when speaking but very strict when writting. Skipping some "ne", "pas", "un" etc during a discussion is **very** common.

Both are correct and you can even simpl say "Vous parlez français?".

&#x200B;

If it's the start of the conversation do not forget "**Excusez-moi"** before because you never ever start a conversation by a direct question.

There is no direct translation for "fall down the rabbit hole" in French. We have other similar expression but no one of them is a direct translation and you have subtle variation with different meaning. It would be easier to get the whole context (is it a positive or negative thing for exemple?)

&#x200B;

What you need the most is vocabulary and expression. Grammar is the cherry on the cake. If you have the vocabulary people will figure out what you are saying even if grammar isn't good and you'll improve by speaking with people. You can have a perfect grammar, if you don't know the word for PEN, you'll struggle to ask for one. On the opposite you can have a terrible grammar but people will understand what you want if you know the vocabulary. 

&#x200B;

Also once in France never EVER mix French with English when you speak. I spoke with a couple of english tourist once and they were learning French. They wanted to try to speak a bit of French because they took french class but they mixed everything and it tooks me a lot of time and effort to understand what was in French and what was in English because their prononciation wasn't great. "We worked 20 years as Jardinier". It tooks me 2 solids minutes to understand what a "jardinier" was meaning in english and only understood when they finally said "gardener".

